<!-- ARTICLE -->
<div class="article-body">

  <p><span class="dropcap">I</span>n April 2026, shareholders of Kazakhstan's state-owned uranium giant Kazatomprom voted with a striking 92.9 percent majority to approve a supply contract with India's Department of Atomic Energy. At over $4 billion, covering long-term deliveries of natural uranium concentrates, the deal represents more than half of Kazatomprom's total booked asset value. The vote was not merely a commercial event. It was a statement of geopolitical intent: Astana believes in New Delhi as a long-term strategic partner, and it wanted the world to see that belief reflected in a shareholder register.</p>

  <p>At almost the same moment, India's preferred corridor of Chabahar Port and the INSTC threading through Iran was quietly collapsing. The US sanctions waiver had lapsed. The connecting railway faced delays. The Union Budget had allocated it zero rupees.</p>

  <p class="p-short">India had found its largest uranium partner. It had simultaneously lost its most direct route to that partner's landlocked nation.</p>

