  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="label">01</span>
    Why Kazakhstan?
  </div>

  <p>India's hunger for uranium is a structural feature of its nuclear ambitions, not a recent preference. The country currently operates 24 reactors at roughly 8 GW. Its declared target is 100 GW by 2047. Between 2025 and 2033 alone, the government estimates it will need to import at least 9,000 metric tonne units of uranium to fuel reactors under construction or advanced planning. Domestic mines, primarily in Jharkhand's Singhbhum belt, produce approximately 400 tonnes annually ,enough to cover a fraction of what the expansion demands. India's own reserves are low-grade, expensive to extract, and capable of meeting only about 25 percent of future demand at the outside.</p>

  <p>Kazakhstan is the natural answer. It accounts for over 40 percent of global uranium output, holds approximately 14 percent of known world reserves, and employs in-situ recovery methods that make its production costs among the lowest on earth. The 2026 deal follows earlier agreements in 2009 and 2015, but the scale is of a different order. This is not a supplementary supply arrangement, It is a foundational partnership, the sort that takes years of patient diplomacy to assemble and a single political crisis to unravel.</p>

  <p>For Kazakhstan, the maths is equally clear. Chinese companies have been steadily acquiring stakes in Kazakhstani uranium projects, quietly displacing positions previously held by Russia's Rosatom. Diversifying its customer base, anchoring a long-term relationship with a major democratic power that has its own sovereign nuclear ambitions, is precisely what Astana's multi-vector foreign policy doctrine demands. The 92.9 percent shareholder approval is not a boardroom vote. It is a nation-level bet.</p>

  <div class="observation">
    There is something worth noting in that number. A margin that large, on a deal that size, does not happen without deliberate government guidance. Kazakhstan did not just accept India's offer rather it chose it.
