  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="label">02</span>
    Bhabha's Gambit — What India Actually Intends to Do with This Uranium
  </div>

  <p>To understand why the Kazakhstan deal matters beyond its headline value, you need to understand what India plans to do with the uranium. And that requires a brief encounter with the most architecturally ambitious nuclear fuel cycle ever conceived by any country.</p>

  <p>In the 1950s, physicist Homi Jehangir Bhabha designed a three-stage nuclear programme explicitly engineered around India's resource reality: limited uranium, but one of the world's largest deposits of thorium, estimated at over a million tonnes, theoretically enough to power the country for centuries. The logic was elegant in its patience: use imported and domestic uranium in the first stage, use the plutonium that generates to breed more fuel in the second, and unlock the thorium cycle in the third. Each stage feeds the next. The whole structure only works if the first stage runs reliably, indefinitely.</p>

  <!-- THREE STAGE TABLE -->
  <table class="stage-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Stage</th><th>Reactor Type</th><th>Fuel Input</th><th>Output / Purpose</th><th>Status</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="stage-badge">I</span></td>
        <td>Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs)</td>
        <td>Natural uranium (imported + domestic)</td>
        <td>Electricity + plutonium-239 from spent fuel</td>
        <td><span class="status-active">● Operational</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="stage-badge">II</span></td>
        <td>Fast Breeder Reactors (FBRs)</td>
        <td>Plutonium-239 (from Stage I reprocessing)</td>
        <td>More fuel than consumed; breeds U-233 from thorium blanket</td>
        <td><span class="status-progress">◐ Just Entered — PFBR, Kalpakkam, April 2026</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="stage-badge">III</span></td>
        <td>Advanced Heavy Water Reactors (AHWRs)</td>
        <td>Thorium-232 + Uranium-233</td>
        <td>Near-inexhaustible closed fuel cycle using thorium</td>
        <td><span class="status-future">○ Future — Under Development</span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Most commentary on the Kazakhstan deal treats it as a simple supply transaction. That misses the point. Every kilogram of natural uranium fed into a PHWR generates the plutonium that will fuel the Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor at Kalpakkam, which achieved first criticality in April 2026, making India only the second country after Russia to operate a commercial fast breeder. That plutonium, in turn, will breed uranium-233 from thorium blankets, unlocking Stage III. Without a secure, diversified uranium supply pipeline, Bhabha's entire architecture stalls at the first rung. The Kazatomprom contract is not merely a supply deal.</p>

  <p class="p-short">India is not importing uranium. It is purchasing the raw material for its own nuclear independence — a distinction that separates resource diplomacy from resource dependency.</p>

  <p>The Kazakhstan deal is also notable for what it signals about India's broader procurement strategy. New Delhi has simultaneously signed a CAD 2.6 billion deal with Canada's Cameco for nearly 22 million pounds of uranium concentrate over nine years beginning 2027. Russia and France remain in the supplier portfolio. This is deliberate multi-sourcing, the same logic that governs India's defence procurement. No single nation should hold veto power over its nuclear fuel supply chain.</p>

