  <p>The International North-South Transport Corridor — a 7,200-kilometre multimodal network connecting Mumbai through Iran, the Caspian Sea, and onward to Russia, Central Asia, and Northern Europe, was designed to be precisely the infrastructure that would make India's presence in Kazakhstan meaningful beyond a single supply contract. Estimates suggested it could cut transit times by 40 percent and logistics costs by 30 percent compared to Suez Canal routes. Chabahar Port, operated by India Ports Global with a $120 million investment, was its southern anchor.</p>

  <p>That anchor has come loose. The assassination of Iran's Supreme Leader in February 2026 and the subsequent US-Israel-Iran military conflict have fundamentally altered the operating environment. The Chabahar-Zahedan railway, critical for connecting the port to Iran's national grid and slated for completion in 2026, now faces indefinite delays as Iran redirects resources to wartime mobilisation. The sanctions waiver expired with no extension. The most telling detail was not the military conflict at all but it was the budget line. The Union Budget 2026-27 allocated zero rupees to Chabahar. A government communicates its priorities through what it funds and what it quietly abandons.</p>

  <div class="observation">
    Geopolitical strategist Brahma Chellaney warned that India's retreat would directly benefit China's connectivity ambitions in the region. Gwadar Port — Beijing's CPEC anchor — sits just 170 kilometres from Chabahar. The geography is unforgiving: if India withdraws, it does not leave a vacuum. It leaves a prize.
  </div>

