  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="label">05</span>
    What New Delhi Must Do — Three Imperatives
  </div>

  <ul class="rec-list">
    <li data-n="1">
      <strong>Do not abandon Chabahar — restructure it</strong>
      A full Indian withdrawal from Chabahar hands Beijing a strategic gift it has sought for years. New Delhi should explore a temporary transfer of terminal operations to an Iranian entity under a legal framework that preserves India's contractual rights, maintain back-channel engagement with Tehran regardless of Washington's posture, and keep the option to re-engage operationally when the conflict environment permits. Strategic autonomy is not only claimed in votes at the UN , it is exercised in port contracts during inconvenient moments.
    </li>
    <li data-n="2">
      <strong>Accelerate the IMEC and Caspian bypass routes</strong>
      The India-Middle East-Europe Corridor offers a partial alternative that avoids Iranian territory. Simultaneously, the Caspian sea route component of INSTC — connecting Kazakhstan and Turkmenistan to Baku — should be prioritised as an Iran-independent segment capable of carrying meaningful cargo volumes. India should put financial and diplomatic weight behind both, not just diplomatic language.
    </li>
    <li data-n="3">
      <strong>Upgrade the Kazakhstan relationship to a technology partnership</strong>
      The $4 billion uranium contract is a floor, not a ceiling. India should open formal dialogue on nuclear fuel cycle cooperation: joint ventures in uranium processing, potential SMR collaboration, and Central Asia's first India-backed rare earth processing facility. Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan together hold not just uranium but beryllium, tantalum, and rare earth elements that India's semiconductor and defence industries will increasingly need. A supply chain anchored in technology partnership survives political weather far better than one anchored in price.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- CONCLUSION -->
  <div class="section-head">
    <span class="label">Conclusion</span>
    The Stress Test of Strategic Patience
  </div>

  <p>The Kazatomprom deal is genuinely significant , as a symbol of India's growing gravitational pull in Eurasian resource markets, as a foundation for the Bhabha nuclear architecture, and as evidence that state-backed diplomacy, executed with patience, can produce agreements that no private company could anchor. Astana's 92.9 percent vote is not just an endorsement of a commercial deal. It is a statement about which powers Central Asia wants to count among its long-term partners.</p>

  <p>But the simultaneous retreat from Chabahar threatens to hollow out that signal. An India that wins uranium contracts while abandoning port investments sends an uncomfortable message: that its engagement with the region is extractive rather than constructive, interested in resources but not relationships. That is precisely the characterisation China's Belt and Road narrative has historically attached to India, and it would be a costly self-inflicted wound to validate it through inaction.</p>

  <p>Homi Bhabha designed his three-stage programme in the 1950s with the confidence that India would have the strategic depth to see it through across decades, governments, and geopolitical disruptions. That confidence is now being tested not in a laboratory but in the unglamorous terrain of port contracts, corridor budgets, and EGM vote tallies.</p>

  <p class="p-short">Whether India's policymakers match Bhabha's long-term vision in their current decisions will determine not just the country's energy future, but the credibility of its claim to be a consequential Eurasian power. The uranium is secured. The road, for now, is broken. The question is what India does next.</p>

